menu "espix processes"

    config ESPIX_PROC_MAX
        int "Maximum tracked processes"
        range 2 64
        default 32
        help
            Size of the espix process table: the limit on processes alive at
            once. A finished process copies what a listing needs into the
            completed log (see espix_proc_history()) and releases its slot, so
            this bounds concurrency and nothing else.

            A slot is about 224 bytes of internal RAM. What only an ELF app
            needs -- its image, argv, environment and paths -- is a block in
            PSRAM it gets at spawn, so daemons and kernel threads, which have
            pids too, cost the slot alone.

    config ESPIX_PTHREAD_MAX
        int "Maximum threads, across all processes"
        range 4 64
        default 32
        help
            Size of the table that ties a thread to its process -- an app's
            pthreads, and espix's own (sshd:rx, audio's mixer) -- so a signal
            reaches them and the process's end can end them. One table for the
            whole system, 20 bytes an entry; pthread_create beyond it fails with
            EAGAIN rather than starting a thread nobody can reach.

    config ESPIX_PROC_STACK_SIZE
        int "Default stack size for a loaded app (bytes)"
        range 2048 524288
        default 8192
        help
            Stack given to the task that relocates and runs an ELF app. Apps
            run on this stack, so it must accommodate the deepest call chain
            the app makes, including printf.

    config ESPIX_PROC_PRIORITY
        int "Priority for loaded app tasks"
        range 1 10
        default 3
        help
            Above the console session, which runs on main_task at priority 1,
            so a newly created app preempts the shell that started it. proc_task
            waits on the process lock at entry because of that; see the comment
            there before changing this.

    config ESPIX_PROC_ABI_WATCHPOINT
        bool "Watch the ABI resolver state for stray writes"
        default y
        help
            Arm a hardware watchpoint on the symbol resolver's table state, so a
            write to it from anywhere panics at the instruction that did it.

            This exists because that state was found corrupted on a running
            board: apps could no longer resolve getenv(), which the resolver
            publishes, while getpid() from the table registered before it still
            worked. The resolver had been left searching one table instead of
            two, and it stayed that way until reboot.

            s_table_count sits immediately after g_espix_procs in .bss, so a
            single word written one past the process table lands on it. Nothing
            rewrites it afterwards, so the damage is silent and permanent, and
            it surfaces an hour later as a missing symbol.

            Costs one of the ESP32-S3's two watchpoints. Turn it off only if
            something else needs both.

    config ESPIX_PROC_IMAGE_IN_PSRAM
        bool "Read app images into PSRAM when available"
        default y
        help
            Allocate the buffer holding the ELF file from PSRAM, falling back to
            internal RAM. Independent of ELF_LOADER_LOAD_PSRAM, which controls
            where the *relocated* image lives.

endmenu
